## Structural Variations of Command Injection Operators
Security testing methodologies typically classify payloads based on how the operating system's command interpreter handles syntax tokenization. When an application attempts to sanitize input using simple blocklists, variations in syntax structure determine whether a command is processed.
## 1. Alternative Command Separators
If a filter strictly blocks a semicolon (%3B), alternative operators that define command boundaries within shell syntax include:

* Newlines (%0A or \n): In Linux shells, a newline character acts as a complete command terminator, identical to a semicolon.
* Carriage Returns (%0D or \r): Often paired with newlines in Windows environments to separate commands. [1] 

## 2. Handling Spaces without Whitespace Characters
Filters often look for standard spaces (%20 or +) to identify multi-word commands. Shell interpreters allow alternative internal field separators (IFS) to distinguish arguments: [2] 

* The $IFS Variable (Linux): The shell uses the IFS environment variable to determine word boundaries. Input like sleep$IFS5 uses the variable instead of a space character. [3, 4] 
* Redirection Operators: Characters like < can sometimes separate commands and paths in specific shell contexts because the shell structurally expects an input source.

