## Designing Robust Protections Against Bypasses
Blocklist-based filters (searching for specific characters like ; or `) are generally ineffective because shell syntax offers numerous encoding and structural alternatives. To build a secure system that cannot be bypassed, developers implement structural validation rather than character filtering. [5, 6, 7, 8] 
## Robust Allowlisting [9] 
Instead of attempting to block dangerous characters, validate that the input contains only safe characters. [10] 

<?php
$user_code = $_GET['code'] ?? '';

// Allow ONLY alphanumeric characters. No spaces, no operators, no bypass variations.
if (!preg_match('/^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$/', $user_code)) {
    http_response_code(400);
    die("Invalid input structure.");
}

// The validated string is now safe to process
?>

## Total Avoidance of Shells
The only definitive defense against command injection variations is to ensure user input never reaches a shell interpreter (/bin/sh, cmd.exe, powershell.exe). If system utilities must be called, use programming language APIs that execute binaries directly without invoking a shell context, passing arguments as elements in a distinct array rather than a single concatenated string. [11, 12] 
